WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.620
Today, we have something much requested by
the audience.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:14.000
We are going to be speaking with Varsh, not
Vash, not the oche, but Varsh, a popular streamer

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.690
debater, progressive socialist with nearly
300000 subscribers.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:24.920
Now, I think it's important my audience understands
that I don't call people socialists when they're

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.000
not socialist.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.780
You're an actual bona fide socialist.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.840
Yeah, I'd like to think I am at least cool.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.279
Well, we'll get into that.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:33.279
And now what?

00:00:33.279 --> 00:00:36.730
I'm always curious, any time I deal with anybody
who uses a pseudonym.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.690
How did you decide to operate under a pseudonym?

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.170
I was a big old art kid back in high school.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.630
You know, every art kid wants to draw comics,
or at least a lot of them do.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.360
OK is the name of a character that I wanted
to draw.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.420
Anyway, when I started doing the content,
I just decided to use it as a placeholder

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.719
name and it stuck.

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:55.850
So that's catchy enough.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.900
And I'm guessing for privacy reasons, it's
not the worst thing.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:00:59.900
Oh, sure.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.500
Yeah, but I mean, people go either way, but
I think it's nice and catchy anyway, so I

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.500
don't mind.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.850
So let's let's talk a little bit.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.340
I mean, maybe to get into our conversation,
we are coming off the heels of an election

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.990
in which Donald Trump lost Joe Biden won Senate
control.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:18.729
Is that at this point?

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:23.020
And we're sort of trying to figure out what
what is the next step for someone with your

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.540
perspective as a socialist, where both Joe
Biden and Donald Trump are very different

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.200
from you in terms in terms of their politics?

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.010
Do you see replacing Trump with Biden as a
good thing?

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.729
How good of a thing?

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:39.930
Oh, undeniably, this is something that I learned
when I looked into the writings of people

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.220
like Engels and Lenin and Mao.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.790
Is he socialists are, at least, as I understand
it, most comfortable making our arguments

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.799
in a democratic or a bourgeois democratic
system.

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:55.659
That is to say, in a system where we have
sort of this veneer of corporate democracy,

00:01:55.659 --> 00:02:00.810
where people expect things like rights and
representation, you can make arguments like,

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.149
hey, couldn't we be doing this better in outright
fascist societies or societies which have

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:09.530
become so nationalistic that national interest
takes precedence over class interest?

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.110
It's really difficult to get those arguments
across.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.701
That's why we typically lose whenever fascists
take power, we're the first ones out, we're

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:17.870
the biggest threat.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:24.550
So preserving the system for a little bit
longer under Joe Biden, vastly, vastly preferable

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:28.220
to the threat of our democratic institutions
crumbling.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.790
Also, just frankly, Biden's just better look
objectively at a number of issues.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.099
Climate change, health care.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:34.100
Can't argue with that.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:39.459
So very clearly, you're making you're making
it obvious that you are not an acceleration

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:44.599
as the acceleration ism being the idea that
there would be a shortcut to your eventual

00:02:44.599 --> 00:02:51.020
ideal arrangement of of affairs by having
society crumble, by having those institutions

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.080
crumble, that that would in some way accelerate
or more quickly get us to that endpoint.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.140
You very clearly don't subscribe to that.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.750
The conditions need to be right for that.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.760
If things collapsed right now, we would not
be the ones to take power.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.340
We do not have that kind of infrastructure
established.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.600
They would be the ones to take power in a
system where it felt like chaos or collapse

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:16.760
would be something which would inspire some
kind of, you know, natural, organic, democratic

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:17.760
control.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.760
I could get behind that.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.420
But that is not the world we live in right
now.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:24.959
What would happen in your mind if things crumbled
instead of a socialist utopia?

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:26.480
What would we most likely see?

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.480
Oh, man.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.500
I mean, if things I mean, depending on how
things crumbled right now, the general prevailing

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.099
attitude in this country is fear and nationalism.

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:37.540
We would probably set up some sort of, if
not one singular, at least a collection of

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.989
ethno nationalist communities.

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:44.920
The direction this country is taking right
now, the world is controlled, at least in

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.629
chaos, by the most militarily prepared.

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:52.430
And right now, I think a large number of Trump
supporters are ready.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:58.019
They believe they're on the precipice of having
to take hold of a dead world, whereas a lot

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:02.030
of progressive and left leaning people, you
know, God bless them, whatever, I don't think

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.220
are ready for that.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:06.970
And for that reason, if push comes to shove,
I'm just not confident that our chances I

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.300
think that we're going to go dramatically
far.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.300
Right.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.520
I think we're probably going to turn into
an isolationist ethno nationalist state and

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.190
from that point forward will be completely
unable to affect the rest of the world as

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.299
its course changes.

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:19.579
And I don't I don't want that.

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:20.579
Yeah, OK.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:22.049
So so far, I completely agree with you.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:26.590
I think that if we do see a crumbling that
some imagine might lead to the ushering in

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:31.900
of the idealized socialist circumstances,
I think we get exactly what you're talking

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.330
about now.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:41.500
Under what circumstances do you believe that
your socialist organization of the country

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.190
would be most likely to be achieved?

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:48.810
What would happen between now and then leading
up to it?

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.949
We need to do a lot of cultural work and we
need to get people to.

00:04:52.949 --> 00:04:55.610
Fixate on issues that actually affect their
lives.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.110
What gets a Trump supporter out of bed, what
gets them angry at the world, is that the

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:03.460
state of their schools, their infrastructure,
their education, their health care.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.460
Very rarely.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.190
No, usually it's meaningless cultural issues.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.190
You have trans people.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.050
You have the belief that BLM is going to burn
down their suburb.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.710
These are nonsense, scaremongering, fear issues.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.560
These aren't reflective of reality.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.050
And for that reason, this is why we are concern
ourselves with the spread of something called

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.160
class consciousness.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.080
We want people to be concerned first and foremost
with the conditions that actually affect their

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.080
lives.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.699
And if we can do that, and I believe we can
do that and people like Bernie Sanders have

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:33.259
shown you can do that to lots of people if
you focus on the right arguments, I think

00:05:33.259 --> 00:05:37.610
that will be in a position where the predominant
ideological trend of this country will be

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:43.949
one towards radical workplace democracy as
opposed to radical isolationism, radical ethnic

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:46.660
exclusionary behavior, that sort of thing.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:53.229
Am I being too simplistic if I say that for
the time being, socialists and Social Democrats

00:05:53.229 --> 00:05:57.860
should be united because the next steps are
identical for both groups?

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.419
Or at least they should be?

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:01.050
No, I think that's totally true.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.300
I mean, will backstab each other later, right?

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.300
Strickly?

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.300
Yeah, of course.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:06.300
Of course.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.300
But for right now, yeah.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.960
We need to focus on Social Democrats.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:09.960
You consider yourself one.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:10.960
Of course.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:11.960
I do know the war.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:12.960
Yeah.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.580
Because a senior content, Social Democrats
and socialists have a lot in common and that

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:20.450
we both respect the belief that the economic
resources of our country should be turned

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:21.770
towards the well-being of the people.

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:27.580
We should focus on improving these base institutional
needs that we believe humans are entitled

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:28.580
to.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:29.580
And that's great.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:33.449
And I think that when people are exposed to
that, they're way less likely to associate

00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:37.550
their well-being with these meaningless cultural
platitudes that Trump supporters or other

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:39.860
culture warriors like to obsess over.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.820
For now, I think our interests are aligned
down the line.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:45.130
You know, we'll have our arguments, I'm sure.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.920
So now let's think ahead a little bit.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:53.639
If we just to really kind of continue exploring
your your worldview, imagine that the Social

00:06:53.639 --> 00:06:55.889
Democrats get the things they want.

00:06:55.889 --> 00:06:58.360
We have democratization of the workplace.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:04.330
We are sort of raising the floor and putting
some ceiling in and make sure making sure

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.290
nobody falls too far and doing some of the
things Finland and other northern European

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:09.580
places have done.

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.160
Social Democrats get their way.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.870
OK, once you get to that point.

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:21.150
What mechanism do you imagine would usher
in socialism, how do we get to where every

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.720
business, for example, socializes ownership
of the means of production?

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.960
By what mechanism is that installed or enacted?

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.800
Well, you mentioned workplace democracy, which
I like.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.580
I think those are very good Trojan horse for
socialist ideals, because it literally is

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:40.520
the seizure of the means of production in
a society in which every workplaces democratic.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:44.449
That is to say, there are some there is some
kind of democratic framework that allows you

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:47.440
to vote in or vote out individuals, managers,
that sort of thing.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.509
And the means of those firms, the wealth,
the resources are collectively owned among

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:52.610
those who work there.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:56.979
If you have every firm in a country that adheres
to those principles, you have what would have

00:07:56.979 --> 00:08:01.229
been called one hundred and fifty years ago,
a dictatorship of the proletariat, not very

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:04.919
well named, but essentially because dictatorship
didn't have the connotation back then that

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:07.460
it does now a workers state.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:10.720
Well, let me let's pause for a second there.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.539
Maybe we might have a different idea of what
in fact, it's very clear we have a different

00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:17.180
idea of what democratization of the workplace
would mean.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:21.840
So when I think about democratization of the
workplace, I'm talking about four companies

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.720
above a certain size.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:31.900
You must have broad representation from employees
when you so there's a list of things.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:36.150
But I think it's probably it certainly doesn't
sound like it would go as far as what you're

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.970
talking about, which sounds like it would
almost be a socialization of the means of

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.159
production.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:42.450
That would be the goal for me at least.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:47.930
I think that a democratically minded people
are very susceptible to arguments in favor

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:51.029
of the democratization of the workplace in
the way that I describe it.

00:08:51.029 --> 00:08:55.990
Yeah, I think that once you look at a firm
and you look at its traditional organization,

00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:00.180
which is pretty dictatorial in its natural
state, you know, here in the States, you don't

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.300
control who your manager is or your bosses.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:04.840
You work there and you do what you're told
or you're fired.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:09.440
I think that people are very amicable to the
idea that there can be an improvement on that

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:10.440
system.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.410
There's very, very positive data coming out
of countries well all around the world and

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:18.690
different material conditions suggesting that
worker cooperatives are a pretty effective

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:19.690
way of organizing firms.

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:20.980
And it leaves people happier.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:22.620
And in some cases it's even more efficient.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:28.230
So the question is, how can we sell people
on the idea that that system, which necessitates

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:32.890
the destruction of the bourgeoisie, the class,
you no longer have an owner after all, it's

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:33.890
collective.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:34.890
How do we incentivize that?

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:36.310
How do we encourage people to move in that
direction?

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:43.320
But is your view so there's a dramatic difference
between everybody is convinced and sold on

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:50.170
the idea that that's how businesses should
be owned versus it is imposed as this is how

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:53.300
it must be done or otherwise it is not a legal
business.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.839
It sounds like you're leaning, at least for
now, more in the direction of saying you feel

00:09:56.839 --> 00:10:00.040
like you have the facts on your side and you
could convince people to do it that way.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:04.050
Are you not suggesting that at a certain point
the only legal way to organize a business

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:05.829
would be socialized ownership?

00:10:05.829 --> 00:10:07.950
I don't think you can compel democracy.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.470
I don't think it works very well.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:14.180
I think that when socialism arrives in the
states, should it arrive, nobody will call

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:15.399
it socialism.

00:10:15.399 --> 00:10:19.589
They will view it as a natural extension of
a series of economic and worker related rights

00:10:19.589 --> 00:10:22.610
that they've been building upon it for a good
time before that.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:27.220
With regards to whether or not this should
be pushed for in an autocratic way, though,

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:31.470
an autocratic sort of delivery of democracy
is a little little silly.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:32.470
But it's happened before.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:33.470
Yes.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:37.029
When we freed the slaves, of course, the slave
owners didn't like that, but we did it anyway.

00:10:37.029 --> 00:10:39.100
And I think we can agree that was a good thing.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:42.970
The people who will be pushing back on this
aren't going to be the democratic populace.

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.330
It's probably going to be the owners of these
businesses.

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:46.600
And that's the ultimate conflict.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:54.880
You know, how well how significantly can the
interests of the majority be assigned precedence

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:00.589
over the interests of a very small, very elite
group of landed modern aristocrats?

